-- title:
--   The locally convex inductive topology exists, and a seminorm is continuous for it iff it is continuous on every stage
-- statement:
--   **The locally convex inductive topology and its continuous seminorms.** Let $E$ be a complex vector space, $(E_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ a family of topological vector spaces over $\mathbb C$ (locally convex spaces in the applications) and $f_\alpha\colon E_\alpha\to E$ linear maps. Let $\tau$ be the *locally convex inductive topology* on $E$ defined by the $f_\alpha$: the finest locally convex vector space topology on $E$ for which every $f_\alpha$ is continuous. (For $E=\bigoplus_\alpha E_\alpha$ and the canonical injections this is the locally convex direct sum topology; for $E=\varinjlim E_\alpha$ and the canonical maps it is the locally convex inductive limit topology.)
--
--   Formally, let $\mathcal T$ be the set of all locally convex topologies on $E$ making every $f_\alpha$ continuous, and let $\tau$ be the topology generated by the union of the members of $\mathcal T$, i.e. the coarsest topology finer than every member of $\mathcal T$ (this is `sInf` in Mathlib's order on topologies, where smaller means finer). The theorem states:
--
--   1. $\tau$ is itself a locally convex vector space topology;
--   2. every $f_\alpha\colon E_\alpha\to(E,\tau)$ is continuous (so $\tau$ is the finest element of $\mathcal T$);
--   3. a seminorm $p$ on $E$ is continuous for $\tau$ if and only if $p\circ f_\alpha$ is continuous on $E_\alpha$ for every $\alpha$:
--   $$p\ \text{is }\tau\text{-continuous}\iff p\circ f_\alpha\ \text{is continuous for all }\alpha\in A.$$
--
--   Consequently $\tau$ is the topology defined by the family of all seminorms $p$ on $E$ whose restrictions $p\circ f_\alpha$ to the stages are continuous. The source recalls this construction as the foundation of §C.1.5 (locally convex direct sums, inductive limits, LF-spaces); every later statement about a "locally convex inductive limit topology" rests on it.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** In Mathlib's order on topologies smaller means finer, so `sInf` of a set of topologies is the topology generated by the union of its members; parts 1 and 2 say that this infimum belongs to the set $\mathcal T$, hence is its finest member. The same expression `sInf {t | IsLocallyConvexTopology E t ∧ ...}` is the encoding of inductive topologies used by `MasulloGRH.IsStrictLF` and `MasulloGRH.IsLF`. Local convexity is `MasulloGRH.IsLocallyConvexTopology` (defined by a family of seminorms; no Hausdorff condition). The stages are only assumed to be topological vector spaces; the index type is arbitrary (if it is empty, $\tau$ is the finest locally convex topology of $E$).
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, pp. 662-663, Example C.1.4.6(4) and the opening of §C.1.5; classical reference: H. H. Schaefer with M. P. Wolff, Topological Vector Spaces, Second Edition, Springer, Ch. II, p. 54 (as cited by the paper)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MasulloGRH_LocallyConvex

open Topology

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace MasulloGRH

theorem lcInductiveTopology_isLocallyConvex_and_seminorm_continuous_iff (E : Type*)
    [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℂ E] {ι : Type*} (X : ι → Type*) [∀ a, AddCommGroup (X a)]
    [∀ a, Module ℂ (X a)] [∀ a, TopologicalSpace (X a)] [∀ a, IsTopologicalAddGroup (X a)]
    [∀ a, ContinuousSMul ℂ (X a)] (f : ∀ a, X a →ₗ[ℂ] E) :
    let t : TopologicalSpace E :=
      sInf {t | IsLocallyConvexTopology E t ∧ ∀ a, @Continuous (X a) E _ t (f a)}
    IsLocallyConvexTopology E t ∧ (∀ a, @Continuous (X a) E _ t (f a)) ∧
      ∀ p : Seminorm ℂ E, @Continuous E ℝ t _ p ↔ ∀ a, Continuous (fun x : X a => p (f a x)) := by
  sorry

end MasulloGRH
